WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.128 --> 00:00:05.338
Back when researchers
first analyzed the genome

00:00:05.422 --> 00:00:07.340
of a brand-new coronavirus in Wuhan,

00:00:07.424 --> 00:00:10.135
its genetic sequence looked like this.

00:00:11.302 --> 00:00:14.347
Three weeks later, researchers
sequenced the genome of the same virus

00:00:14.472 --> 00:00:16.182
in a patient in Seattle.

00:00:16.307 --> 00:00:20.145
It had changed
here and here and here.

00:00:20.186 --> 00:00:22.689
These changes are happening
all over the world.

00:00:22.772 --> 00:00:24.733
The coronavirus is mutating.

00:00:24.816 --> 00:00:27.318
It's changed hundreds of times
since January

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:30.780
but researchers are concerned
about one mutation in particular.

00:00:31.156 --> 00:00:33.033
So my question is,

00:00:33.158 --> 00:00:36.036
is the coronavirus
becoming more dangerous?

00:00:36.619 --> 00:00:39.372
I'm Cleo Abram,
and this is "Answered, by Vox."

00:00:41.249 --> 00:00:44.377
Well there's two ways
one can think about danger.

00:00:44.544 --> 00:00:49.382
First is that the virus
causes a more severe disease

00:00:49.466 --> 00:00:51.259
and that doesn't appear to be the case.

00:00:51.384 --> 00:00:54.095
That's professor of immunology
Michael Farzan.

00:00:54.345 --> 00:00:57.807
He says the second way that mutations
could make the virus more dangerous

00:00:57.891 --> 00:01:00.060
is if it becomes more contagious.

00:01:00.351 --> 00:01:02.479
But before we get there,
here's the good news.

00:01:02.562 --> 00:01:05.565
My understanding
is that most mutations,

00:01:05.690 --> 00:01:08.234
in the coronavirus
but also in every virus,

00:01:08.526 --> 00:01:11.696
don't actually change
how it behaves in our bodies

00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:12.947
at all, is that right?

00:01:13.073 --> 00:01:15.575
Yes, most mutations
do not change very much.

00:01:15.658 --> 00:01:18.244
They actually are
just part of the process

00:01:19.245 --> 00:01:22.207
of sort of selection
and they go away.

00:01:22.290 --> 00:01:26.628
A virus, at its most basic,
is just a bunch of genetic material

00:01:26.711 --> 00:01:28.421
packed into a protein shell.

00:01:28.505 --> 00:01:31.966
In the case of the coronavirus,
that genetic material is RNA,

00:01:32.092 --> 00:01:34.511
which is made up of four
nucleotide bases.

00:01:34.594 --> 00:01:37.764
You might remember the bases
A, C, T, G from DNA.

00:01:37.847 --> 00:01:40.141
With RNA it's just U, not T.

00:01:40.266 --> 00:01:44.145
When the virus gets into your body,
its goal is to copy itself

00:01:44.270 --> 00:01:48.024
which means it needs to produce
more RNA and more proteins.

00:01:48.149 --> 00:01:51.319
A virus doesn't always make
an exact copy of its RNA.

00:01:51.444 --> 00:01:54.322
And when it makes a random mistake
in that copying process,

00:01:54.447 --> 00:01:55.782
that's a mutation.

00:01:55.865 --> 00:01:59.494
But it's when it's copying the proteins
that things really get interesting.

00:01:59.577 --> 00:02:01.579
Within that newly copied RNA,

00:02:01.663 --> 00:02:04.833
each group of three nucleotides,
called a codon,

00:02:04.916 --> 00:02:08.169
can instruct your cells to produce
one building block of protein

00:02:08.253 --> 00:02:09.712
called an amino acid.

00:02:09.796 --> 00:02:12.006
Amino acids all come
with letter names too:

00:02:12.048 --> 00:02:14.592
D, G, Y, I--
you get the idea.

00:02:14.676 --> 00:02:17.971
Different amino acid combinations
mean different proteins.

00:02:18.012 --> 00:02:19.472
But as it turns out...

00:02:19.556 --> 00:02:23.935
Several different codons encode
for the same amino acid.

00:02:24.018 --> 00:02:27.730
So, for example,
say the nucleotides "ACU"

00:02:27.814 --> 00:02:29.941
mutated into "ACG."

00:02:30.024 --> 00:02:34.654
It's still gonna tell your cell to produce
the exact same amino acid, T.

00:02:34.737 --> 00:02:37.615
This mutation doesn't change
the protein at all.

00:02:37.699 --> 00:02:39.367
We call those silent mutations.

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:43.746
But even if it's a non-silent mutation,
something that does actually

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:46.666
change the amino acid
and changes the protein,

00:02:46.749 --> 00:02:51.296
that doesn't always necessarily change
how a virus behaves in our bodies, right?

00:02:51.379 --> 00:02:53.339
That's absolutely right.
In fact,

00:02:53.381 --> 00:02:56.009
the genetic code is designed
to be conservative.

00:02:56.050 --> 00:02:59.304
Meaning that changes
that it's likely to make,

00:02:59.387 --> 00:03:03.766
are likely to have a minor effect
on the function

00:03:03.850 --> 00:03:05.351
of the proteins they encode for.

00:03:05.393 --> 00:03:08.354
So in general,
mutations are expected

00:03:08.646 --> 00:03:11.357
in the course of an epidemic
or pandemic.

00:03:12.192 --> 00:03:15.820
Most of these mutations
do not have a strong impact

00:03:16.196 --> 00:03:19.032
on the severity of the virus
or even the transmission of the virus.

00:03:19.073 --> 00:03:21.492
Of course,
that's not always the case.

00:03:21.868 --> 00:03:23.244
And for COVID-19,

00:03:23.369 --> 00:03:25.747
scientists are concerned about
one mutation in particular

00:03:25.830 --> 00:03:28.666
that might have an impact
on how the virus spreads.

00:03:28.750 --> 00:03:32.045
The formal name is D614G

00:03:32.253 --> 00:03:36.925
and that is a code for
the individual amino acids

00:03:37.008 --> 00:03:41.262
at position 614 of this spike protein
that have changed.

00:03:41.346 --> 00:03:45.099
I see, so it's the 614th amino acid

00:03:45.767 --> 00:03:48.603
- and it changes from D to G?
- Correct.

00:03:48.686 --> 00:03:51.814
Unfortunately D614G
just so happens to be

00:03:51.898 --> 00:03:53.650
in the part of the genetic sequence

00:03:53.733 --> 00:03:56.027
that encodes for
the viral spike proteins,

00:03:56.110 --> 00:03:59.530
those key proteins that the coronavirus
uses to invade cells.

00:03:59.614 --> 00:04:01.366
The red protein that everybody sees

00:04:01.449 --> 00:04:03.660
on the surface of
the pictures of the coronavirus,

00:04:03.743 --> 00:04:07.163
there's more of those on viruses
with that change

00:04:07.789 --> 00:04:09.582
than on viruses without this change.

00:04:09.666 --> 00:04:12.669
Dr. Farzan and his team
found that with more spiked proteins,

00:04:12.752 --> 00:04:16.089
the mutated virus is more likely
to infect cells

00:04:16.130 --> 00:04:18.007
in culture in their lab.

00:04:18.091 --> 00:04:21.010
But infecting cells in culture
is really very different

00:04:21.094 --> 00:04:24.722
from it being more infectious
out in the world between people, right?

00:04:24.806 --> 00:04:27.934
It is certainly more likely
to infect a cell in culture.

00:04:29.477 --> 00:04:32.480
What the next step is to demonstrate

00:04:33.022 --> 00:04:35.775
that our results in cell culture

00:04:35.858 --> 00:04:38.903
translate to human-to-human transmission.

00:04:38.987 --> 00:04:42.198
So just to be super clear,
we don't know that this mutation

00:04:42.323 --> 00:04:45.285
causes the virus to pass more easily
between humans.

00:04:45.326 --> 00:04:49.122
It does seem to be more transmissible
between cells in a lab

00:04:49.163 --> 00:04:52.625
and it also seems to become
the dominant form of the virus

00:04:52.709 --> 00:04:53.918
as it spreads.

00:04:54.002 --> 00:04:57.297
Where is this mutated version
of the coronavirus now?

00:04:57.338 --> 00:04:58.756
Actually, everywhere.

00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:02.802
The first detection of this virus
might have been in Germany in late January

00:05:03.177 --> 00:05:06.014
and then you would see that virus
sort of grow up in Europe

00:05:06.097 --> 00:05:09.350
and you would see
a mixture of viruses in the United States

00:05:09.434 --> 00:05:13.187
but then over time,
you would see more and more virus

00:05:13.313 --> 00:05:14.647
with this mutation.

00:05:14.731 --> 00:05:17.692
So what we know right now
is that researchers believe

00:05:17.775 --> 00:05:21.946
the mutations we're seeing aren't making
cases of the coronavirus more severe.

00:05:22.322 --> 00:05:25.366
They are concerned that
the D614G gene mutation

00:05:25.491 --> 00:05:27.660
might make the virus
more contagious.

00:05:27.744 --> 00:05:30.788
But the research so far
is limited to cells in a lab.

00:05:30.955 --> 00:05:33.124
And scientists just aren't sure yet

00:05:33.207 --> 00:05:36.419
how the mutation will affect
contagion rates in the real world.

00:05:36.586 --> 00:05:39.505
And if you just remember one thing,
it's this:

00:05:39.589 --> 00:05:42.008
does what we know about this mutation

00:05:42.091 --> 00:05:43.843
change anything that people
should be doing

00:05:43.926 --> 00:05:47.096
in their daily lives to prevent
themselves and their loved ones

00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:49.849
from getting or transmitting coronavirus?

00:05:49.932 --> 00:05:53.895
No, it's just a reminder that
this is a very transmissible virus.

00:05:53.978 --> 00:05:55.021
It always has been.

00:05:55.104 --> 00:05:57.273
It might have gotten
a little bit more transmissible

00:05:57.815 --> 00:06:00.902
and you should be very careful

00:06:00.985 --> 00:06:05.031
at every point to make sure
that people are protected.

00:06:08.534 --> 00:06:09.494
Thanks for watching.

00:06:09.660 --> 00:06:12.455
That was an episode of Vox's
first ever daily show.

